    "abstract": "Global warming is reshaping precipitation patterns worldwide, with arid regions showing particularly striking changes. Lanzhou, a dry and rain-scarce area in northwest China, has experienced increasing precipitation and atmospheric moisture levels amid global warming. Research indicates this \u201cwarm and humid\u201d phenomenon represents an improvement in arid regions \u2018moisture conditions without altering their fundamental arid characteristics. The precipitation patterns and properties in Lanzhou differ fundamentally from those in eastern China, demonstrating that agricultural ecological challenges in arid areas cannot be resolved merely by increasing water availability. \u201cHowever, despite the increase in precipitation, arid regions cannot rely solely on the increase of water resources to address agroecological challenges; the uneven temporal and spatial distribution of rainfall must also be taken into account.\u201d",
    "field": "climate change",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge that increased precipitation in arid Lanzhou does not resolve agroecological issues because its uneven temporal and spatial distribution creates new flood risks, complicating water management in a region that remains fundamentally dry.",
    "method": "This study proposes a quantitative analysis of historical climate data, using trend and correlation analysis, to clarify long-term precipitation changes in Lanzhou and support adaptive water management and regional sustainable development.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze long-term precipitation trends in Lanzhou and the Yellow River Basin using historical data to clarify the mechanisms of change under global warming and provide support for adaptive water management strategies.",
